<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Lebanon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> is a mountain range running parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of Lebanon, and the name of the historic region in its highlands. Its valleys gave refuge to many communities, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronite Christians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a>. In antiquity the Ituraeans held parts of it, and in 1861 it became an autonomous Ottoman district, the core of the later Lebanese state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon.jpg" alt="Mount Lebanon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Linaduliban at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bsharridistrictmountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mount Lebanon range runs about 170 kilometres along the whole country, parallel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, with its highest peak, Qurnat as Sawda', at 3,088 metres. The name Lebanon comes from a Semitic root meaning white, likely a reference to the snow-covered tops. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> separates it from the Anti-Lebanon Mountains. The mountains long protected the local population. They produced too little wheat, which came from the Beqaa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hawran</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> from the mountain was sold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> to Damascus road crossed the range.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> used the cedar forests to build ships, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> sent cedar wood to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s conquest in 64 BCE the range became part of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. After the 5th century monks following a hermit named Maron came from the Orontes valley and preached in the north. In the late 8th century the Mardaites, sent by the Byzantine emperor to raid Islamic territories in Syria, settled in the north and became part of Maronite society. Muslim ascetics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> lived there from the 7th century, and in the 9th century the Tanoukhiyoun settled the south, converting to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> faith in the 11th.</p><p>In the early 17th century Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-din-ii">Fakhr al-Din II</a>, tax-collector of the Druze Chouf, opened it to Maronite settlement after the Ottoman expedition of 1585. The Battle of Ain Dara in 1711 ended a rivalry between Druze clans with the near-complete exodus of the Yamani Druze. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> the mountain had relative autonomy. From the 18th century Maronite settlement spread into Druze districts, and tensions rose after the peasant uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Kisrawan</a> in 1858. The Druze won militarily, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> intervened for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a> and the Druze, and in 1861 the autonomous district of Mount Lebanon was set up under an international guarantee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Historically the name meant the mountain range, not one administrative territory; the south was known as Jabal al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Shuf</a> and the north as Jabal Kisrawan. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> expanded the borders of Mount Lebanon in 1920 to form Greater Lebanon. The last old-growth groves of the Cedar of Lebanon grow on its high slopes in the Cedars of God World Heritage Site, and Christian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> communities have lived together in the mountains for roughly a thousand years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Lebanon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its fortune rested on two assets: a small natural harbour and the forests of cedar, pine and fir on the slopes of <b>Mount Lebanon</b> behind it, the best timber within reach of treeless Egypt and Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Ottoman rule from 1516 the Druze emir Fakhr al-Din II rose to paramount power until his execution in 1635, and the Shihab clan governed <b>Mount Lebanon</b> until 1830.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Druze moved east to the Hauran in waves from 1685, and after the battle of Ain Dara in 1711 many Yamani Druze followed, laying the foundation of the Jabal al-Druze, while Maronites became the dominant group in <b>Mount Lebanon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Printing in Arabic letters had made little headway among Muslims; the first Arabic presses in the Levant, set up by Christian churches in the 18th century at Aleppo and in the monasteries of <b>Mount Lebanon</b>, printed religious books.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Bosnia resisted Istanbul in 1850–1851, and in <b>Mount Lebanon</b> reform led to the Druze–Maronite conflict of 1860, as France claimed to protect the Maronites and Britain the Druze.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ituraeans, an Arab people, took control of the Beqaa valley and parts of <b>Mount Lebanon</b> and the Hermon in the late 2nd century BCE, as the Seleucid Empire declined.</blockquote><a href="/state/ituraea">Ituraea</a></li></ul></section>
